Here's the approach — use ASP.NET Core's built-in exception handler middleware combined with `IProblemDetailsService`:

**In `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Register Problem Details services
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails(options =>
{
    options.CustomizeProblemDetails = context =>
    {
        context.ProblemDetails.Instance = context.HttpContext.Request.Path;
    };
});

var app = builder.Build();

// Global exception handler — no try-catch needed in endpoints
app.UseExceptionHandler(exceptionApp =>
{
    exceptionApp.Run(async context =>
    {
        var exception = context.Features.Get<Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics.IExceptionHandlerFeature>()?.Error;

        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException   => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Resource not found"),
            ArgumentException      => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Invalid request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError, "An unexpected error occurred")
        };

        context.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;

        var problemDetails = new Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = statusCode == 500 ? null : exception?.Message,
            Instance = context.Request.Path
        };

        await context.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problemDetails, context.RequestAborted);
    });
});

app.UseStatusCodePages(); // Handles non-exception error status codes as Problem Details

// Example endpoint — no try-catch needed
app.MapGet("/items/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    if (id < 0) throw new ArgumentException("ID must be positive");
    if (id == 0) throw new KeyNotFoundException("Item not found");
    if (id == 99) throw new InvalidOperationException("Item is locked");
    return Results.Ok(new { Id = id, Name = "Widget" });
});

app.Run();
```

**Key points:**

| Exception | Status | Detail exposed? |
|-----------|--------|-----------------|
| `KeyNotFoundException` | 404 | Yes |
| `ArgumentException` | 400 | Yes |
| `InvalidOperationException` | 409 | Yes |
| Anything else | 500 | **No** (prevents leaking internals) |

**How it works:**
1. `UseExceptionHandler` catches all unhandled exceptions globally
2. The switch expression maps exception types → HTTP status codes
3. For 500s, `Detail` is set to `null` so stack traces/sensitive info never leak
4. Every error response is a valid RFC 7807 JSON body (`application/problem+json`)

**Sample 404 response:**
```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.5",
  "title": "Resource not found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "Item not found",
  "instance": "/items/0"
}
```

No try-catch needed in any endpoint — the middleware handles everything centrally.
